          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            of “test objects”, one can consider “things” which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            can detect. If one already has a class of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "-structures" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed "-objects" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
          #IsbellEnvelope.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} essentially small
              category. Then the
              %em Isbell envelope
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(\mathcal{T})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = (P,F,c)
              where
            %ol
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contravariant+functor"} contravariant functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} \to Set
                (a
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+functor"} covariant functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} \to Set
                (a
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaf"} copresheaf
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo ×
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c : P \times F \to \mathcal{T}(-,-)
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
                of bifunctors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒯
                        %mi op
                      %mo ×
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}^{op} \times \mathcal{T} \to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %p
              (We conventionally write
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = (P_X, F_X, c_X)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
              is a pair of natural transformations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo ,
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\alpha,\beta)
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : P_X \to P_Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta : F_Y \to F_X
              which satisfy the relation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi β
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_X(-,\beta -) = c_Y(\alpha -,-)
            %p
              Abstractly, this is (a reformulation of) the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/envelope+of+an+adjunction"} envelope
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
              adjunction associated to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
          %p
            The requirement that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            be essentially small implies that the collections of natural transformations form sets, and thus that this is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small category
          %p Certain elementary properties are easy to prove.
          #ElemProp.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              There is a (“double/twosided Yoneda”) embedding of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              as a full subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(\mathcal{T})
              via
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    T \mapsto (\mathcal{T}(-,T), \mathcal{T}(T,-), \circ)
            %p
              Identifying
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              with its image, there are
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{T}^g(T,X) \cong P_X(T), \qquad \mathcal{T}^g(X,T) \cong F_X(T)
          %p
            %em Other elementary properties to follow
          %h2#profunctors Profunctors
          %p
            The Isbell envelope of a category can be viewed as a category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            consists of the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                lax factorisations of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Hom
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom
                through
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            Let us spell this out. Recall that a profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}
            is a functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{B}^{op} \to Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            are special examples of profunctors: a covariant functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔉
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F} : \mathcal{A} \to Set
            is a profunctor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} \to 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is the terminal category, and a contravariant functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔊
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{G} : \mathcal{B} \to Set
            is a profunctor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to \mathcal{B}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                    %mo ×
                    %mi 𝔊
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F} \times \mathfrak{G} : \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{B}^{op} \to Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            produces a profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{T}
            which factors through
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            There is an obvious profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{T}
            given by the
            = succeed "-bifunctor." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            defines a morphism of profunctors from the corresponding profunctor to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            corresponds to an object in the subcategory of the slice category of profunctors over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom
            of those objects which factor through
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            In other words, a lax factorisation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom
            through
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            This characterization relates directly to a definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+completion"} Cauchy completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_X
            is the counit of an adjunction. This exhibits the Cauchy completion as a subcategory of the Isbell envelope, that factorizes through both the free completion and free cocompletion:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo ↪
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} \hookrightarrow \bar{\mathcal{T}} \hookrightarrow E(\mathcal{T})
          %h2#concrete_envelopes Concrete Envelopes
          %p
            A variant of the above involves a background category, say
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            defines a profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{T}
            (which factors through
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ×
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝒯
                            %mi op
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %mi 𝒰
                          %mo ×
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝒰
                            %mi op
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %mi Set
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                          %mi 𝒰
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ×
                          %mi 𝒰
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo ,
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{T}^{op} &amp;\to \mathcal{U} \times \mathcal{U}^{op} &amp;&amp;\to Set \\
                  (T_1, T_2) &amp;\mapsto (|T_1|, |T_2|) &amp;&amp;\mapsto \mathcal{U}(U,|T_1|) \times \mathcal{U}(|T_2|,U)
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            Then one can consider those objects of the Isbell envelope of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            that are sub-profunctors of one of this type. In addition one should restrict the morphisms to those that are induced by morphism of objects of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
          #ConcIsbellEnvelope.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              be categories with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{U}
              which we shall write as
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo ↦
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \mapsto |T|
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
                \-concrete Isbell envelope
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi 𝒰
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{\mathcal{U}}(\mathcal{T})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U,P,F)
              where
            %ol
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                is an object of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒰
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P : \mathcal{T} \to Set
                is a subfunctor of the (contravariant) functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi 𝒰
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo ,
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \mapsto \mathcal{U}(|T|,U)
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \mathcal{T} \to Set
                is a subfunctor of the (covariant) functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi 𝒰
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \mapsto \mathcal{U}(U,|T|)
            %p such that the image of the natural transformation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo ×
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    P \times F \to \mathcal{U}(|-|,|-|),
            %p
              which comes from composition in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}(-,-) \to \mathcal{U}(|-|,|-|).
            %p
              A morphism in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{\mathcal{U}}(\mathcal{T})
              is a
              = succeed "-morphism" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              = succeed "-objects" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_X \to P_Y
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_Y \to F_X
          %p
            Having a background category ensures that the size issues with natural transformations do not occur and so we can drop the requirement that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            be essentially small.
          %h2#enrichment Enrichment
          %p
            These notions can be considered in the setting of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            by replacing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            wherever it occurs (explicitly or implicitly) by the enriching category.
          %h2#isbell_duality Isbell Duality
          %p
            Within the Isbell envelope of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            one can consider various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategories"} subcategories
            where the objects satisfy extra conditions. An obvious condition is that the presheaf is actually a sheaf. Another useful condition is that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
          #IsbelDuality.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              An object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(\mathcal{T})
              is said to be
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                \-saturated
              if the obvious natural transformations
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi NatTrans
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    P_X(T) \to NatTrans(F_X,\mathcal{T}(T,-))
            %p
              are isomorphisms. It is said to be
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                \-saturated
              if the obvious natural transformations
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi NatTrans
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F_X(T) \to NatTrans(P_X,\mathcal{T}(-,T))
            %p
              are isomorphisms. It is said to satisfy
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
              if it is both
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              = succeed "-saturated." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            Within
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(\mathcal{T})
            one can consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategories"} full subcategories
            of
            = succeed "-saturated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "-saturated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_X
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X
            by the natural transformations of the other. An interesting question is to ask whether or not the obvious iteration stabilises after a finite number of steps (which would result in an object satisfying Isbell duality).
          %p
            If the test category has, or “morally has”, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            then there is a strong relationship between saturation and concreteness.
          #ConstSep.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em constant separator
              in a category is an object, say
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \ne g : A \to B
              then there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+morphism"} constant morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c : S \to A
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mi c
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi g
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f c \ne g c
          %p
            As the constant morphisms form a two-sided ideal, any object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
            in a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            defines a covariant functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-|_{C_0} : \mathcal{C} \to Set
            which on objects sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to the set of constant morphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0 \to C_0'
            defines a natural transformation (in the opposite direction) between the corresponding functors. From the properties of constant morphisms one can easily deduce that two different morphisms between the same objects induce the same natural transformations between the functors. Thus if there are morphisms between two objects in both directions the two functors are naturally isomorphic.
          %p The property of being a constant separator is clearly equivalent to the condition that this constant functor be faithful. Moreover, it is easy to show that in a non-trivial category, any two constant separators have morphisms between them in both directions and so induce naturally isomorphic functors. Indeed, not just naturally isomorphic but naturally naturally isomorphic in that there is a canonical choice of natural isomorphism.
          %p
            Now we transfer this to the Isbell envelope of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
          #GenSpaceConst.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
